parts.text:
Good, `framer-motion` is available. Now let me implement the changes.

I'll modify `constellation-menu.tsx`:

1. Add state for zooming
2. Add click handler on the center orb div
3. When zooming, render an overlay that zooms the center and fades everything out
4. After animation, navigate to arena

And `renderer.js` to handle the 'arena' section.

Let me write the edits now.

For the constellation menu, I need to:

1. Add state at top: `const [zoomingToArena, setZoomingToArena] = useState(false)`
2. Add useEffect for navigation after animation
3. Make the center orb clickable (add `cursor-pointer` class and `onClick`)
4. Add zoom overlay

Let me also look closely at the center orb's container to decide where to add the onClick. The outer div is:
```tsx
<div className="absolute left-1/2 top-1/2 -translate-x-1/2 -translate-y-1/2"
  style={{ opacity: loadingComplete ? 1 : 0, transition: 'opacity 1.5s ease-in-out' }}
>
```

I'll add `cursor-pointer` and `onClick` to this div. But actually, I need to make sure the click only fires when finishing loading. So I'll put the click handler on the inner div that wraps the SVG and text, or the outer div.

Let me implement:

```tsx
const [zoomingToArena, setZoomingToArena] = useState(false)

const handleCenterClick = () => {
  if (zoomingToArena || !loadingComplete) return
  setZoomingToArena(true)
}

useEffect(() => {
  if (!zoomingToArena) return
  const timer = setTimeout(() => {
    if (window.parent && window.parent !== window) {
      window.parent.postMessage({ type: 'navigate', section: 'arena' }, '*')
    } else {
      window.location.href = '/arena'
    }
  }, 1500)
  return () => clearTimeout(timer)
}, [zoomingToArena])
```

For the center orb, update the outer wrapper:
```tsx
<div
  className="absolute left-1/2 top-1/2 -translate-x-1/2 -translate-y-1/2 cursor-pointer"
  style={{ opacity: loadingComplete ? 1 : 0, transition: 'opacity 1.5s ease-in-out' }}
  onClick={handleCenterClick}
>
```

For the zoom overlay, I'll add it at the end of the JSX (inside the main container but after all other elements, so it renders on top):

```tsx
{zoomingToArena && (
  <motion.div
    className="absolute inset-0 z-50 flex items-center justify-center"
    initial={{ opacity: 0 }}
    animate={{ opacity: 1 }}
    style={{ backgroundColor: '#030a18' }}
  >
    <motion.div
      className="flex flex-col items-center justify-center rounded-full"
      initial={{ scale: 0.2, opacity: 0 }}
      animate={{
        scale: [0.2, 1.5, 3, 6],
        opacity: [0, 1, 1, 0],
      }}
      transition={{ duration: 1.5, ease: "easeInOut", times: [0, 0.3, 0.6, 1] }}
      style={{
        width: centerSize,
        height: centerSize,
        background: "radial-gradient(circle, rgba(72,136,255,0.9) 0%, rgba(72,136,255,0.3) 50%, transparent 70%)",
        border: "2px solid rgba(72, 136, 255, 0.8)",
        boxShadow: "0 0 60px rgba(72, 136, 255, 0.8), 0 0 120px rgba(72, 136, 255, 0.4)",
      }}
    >
      <span className="text-xl font-bold tracking-[0.15em] text-white">KNIRVANA</span>
    </motion.div>
  </motion.div>
)}
```

And I need to also fade the main content (rings, labels, icons) during the zoom. The main container:
```tsx
<div className="relative" style={{ width: 900, height: 900 }}>
```

I should make this a motion.div and fade it when zooming to arena. But that would also fade the zoom overlay since it's inside it. Since the zoom overlay is absolutely positioned on top and the parent fading to 0 shouldn't affect the overlay's own opacity...

Actually, if the parent has `opacity: 0`, the child overlay would also be invisible because opacity is inherited. So I need to put the zoom overlay outside the main content container, or I need to handle the fade differently.

Let me restructure. I'll put the zoom overlay at the same level as the main container, both inside the outer relative wrapper:

```tsx
<div className="relative">
  {/* Starfield */}
  ...

  {/* Main Container - fade everything except the zoom overlay */}
  <motion.div
    className="relative"
    style={{ width: 900, height: 900 }}
    animate={zoomingToArena ? { opacity: 0, scale: 1.5 } : { opacity: 1, scale: 1 }}
    transition={{ duration: 0.8 }}
  >
    {/* SVG Rings, Central Orb, Labels, Icons, etc. */}
  </motion.div>

  {/* Zoom overlay - separate from main content */}
  {zoomingToArena && (
    <motion.div
      className="absolute inset-0 z-50 flex items-center justify-center"
      ...
    >
      ...
    </motion.div>
  )}
</div>
```

Wait, the starfield is also a child of `div className="relative"` which wraps everything. Let me look at the structure more carefully:

```tsx
<div className="relative flex min-h-screen w-full items-center justify-center overflow-hidden"
  style={{ backgroundColor: "#030a18" }}
>
  <div className="relative">
    {/* Starfield */}
    {/* Bottom Horizon Glow */}
    {/* Main Container */}
    <div className="relative" style={{ width: 900, height: 900 }}>
      {/* SVG Rings, Central Orb, Labels, Icons, KNIRV.COM */}
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
```

I think the simplest approach is to put the zoom overlay inside `div className="relative" style={{ width: 900, height: 900 }}` but as the last child (z-index on top). When zooming, I'll fade the existing content (by animating a wrapper around it) and show the zoom overlay on top.

Actually wait - the zoom overlay is positioned `absolute inset-0` which means it's positioned relative to the parent. If the parent is the 900x900 container, then `inset-0` will fill it. But I want the overlay to fill the entire screen, not just the 900x900 container.

Let me rethink. I'll add the zoom overlay at the top level, inside the outermost div:
```tsx
<div className="relative flex min-h-screen w-full ...">
  {/* Existing content */}
  ...

  {/* Zoom overlay - fills entire screen */}
  {zoomingToArena && (
    <motion.div
      className="fixed inset-0 z-50 flex items-center justify-center"
      ...
    >
      <motion.div>...</motion.div>
    </motion.div>
  )}
</div>
```

Using `fixed` instead of `absolute` so it covers the entire viewport.

And for the main content, I'll wrap the existing `div className="relative"` inner wrapper in a motion.div that fades:

```tsx
<motion.div
  className="relative"
  animate={zoomingToArena ? { opacity: 0, scale: 1.2 } : { opacity: 1, scale: 1 }}
  transition={{ duration: 0.8 }}
>
  {/* Starfield */}
  {/* Bottom Horizon Glow */}
  {/* Main Container (rings, labels, icons, center orb) */}
</motion.div>
```

And then the zoom overlay is a sibling of this motion.div.

Let me trace the existing structure:

```tsx
<div className="relative flex min-h-screen ...">   // outerContainer
  {!loadingComplete && <StarSupernova ... />}

  <div className="relative flex min-h-screen ..."> // innerContainer
    <div className="relative">                      // contentWrapper
      {/* Starfield */}
      <div className="absolute inset-0">...</div>

      {/* Bottom Horizon Glow */}
      <div className="pointer-events-none ...">...</div>

      {/* Main Container */}
      <div className="relative" style={{ width: 900, height: 900 }}>
        {/* SVG Rings */}
        {/* Central Orb */}
        {/* Inner Labels */}
        {/* Middle Labels */}
        {/* Outer Icons */}
        {/* KNIRV.COM label */}
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <SettingsModal ... />
</div>
```

So I need to:
1. Wrap `// Starfield`, `// Bottom Horizon Glow`, and `// Main Container` in a motion.div
2. Add the zoom overlay as a sibling inside the innerContainer
3. Make the center orb clickable

Let me write the actual edits now.

